The OS Transport stack (e.g., TCP engine) inspects the Segment:

Port Identification: It reads the Destination Port (e.g., Port 51234) to find which active process or socket owns this traffic.

Sequence & Flow Control (for TCP):

Checks the Sequence Number to place this chunk into the correct order with other received segments.

Sends a TCP ACK back to the sender confirming receipt.

Buffers out-of-order chunks until missing pieces arrive.

De-encapsulation: The TCP header is stripped away once the full payload stream is reassembled.
